<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.1.3: Meeting Constraints</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00373</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.1.3: Meeting Constraints</md:title>
    <md:uuid>faec7853-772f-4402-bfa5-0e513fe54412</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Use the following information to answer 1 - 3.</para>
<para>A dining hall had a total of 25 tables: some long rectangular tables and some round ones. Long tables can seat 8
  people. Round tables can seat 6 people. On a busy evening, all 190 seats at the tables are occupied.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Activity</title><exercise id="_9c791505-e60c-44eb-973f-f57d4b73282f" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>How many long tables \(x\) are there?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 20 long tables</para><para>The correct answer is 20 long tables.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_5fa93333-4840-443d-bc50-6a8ef54eed8b" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>How many round tables, \(y\), are there?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 5 round tables.</para><para>The correct answer is 5 round tables.</para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Q3-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Explain or show how you know how many different size tables there are.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      \(x+y=25\)
      \(8x+6y=190\)
      \(x+y=25\) can be rewritten as \(y=25-x\).
      Substituting \(25-x\) for \(y\) in \(8x+6y=190\) gives \(x=20\).
      If \(x\) is 20, then \(y\) is 5.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>Use the following information to answer 4 - 6.</para>
<para>A family bought a total of 16 adult and child tickets to a magic show. Adult tickets are $10.50 each, and child
  tickets are $7.50 each. The family paid a total of $141.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="cc2d4d6a-4a36-4788-9fc7-3971141afe95" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>How many adult tickets, \(a\) did they buy?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 7 adult tickets</para><para>The correct answer is 7 adult tickets</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_1dd8b612-92ac-4aa7-91a0-4742c2f64e91" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>How many child tickets, \(c\) did they buy?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 9 child tickets.</para><para>The correct answer is 9 child tickets.</para></solution></exercise></section><!--Q6-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Explain or show how you know the prices of the tickets.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      \(a+c=16\)
      \(10.50a+7.50c=141\)
      When the equations are graphed, the intersection of the two lines is at \((7,9)\).</para>
    
    <para>Use the following information to answer 7 - 9</para>
    
    <para>At a poster shop, Han paid $16.80 for 2 large posters and 3 small posters of his favorite band. Kiran paid $14.15
      for 1 large poster and 4 small posters of his favorite TV shows. Posters of the same size have the same price.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>Use the following information to answer 7 - 9</para>
<para>At a poster shop, Han paid $16.80 for 2 large posters and 3 small posters of his favorite band. Kiran paid $14.15 for
  1 large poster and 4 small posters of his favorite TV shows. Posters of the same size have the same price.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="_4714c583-5139-44ca-894f-0c09224a2ae9" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the price of a large poster, \(l\).</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! $4.95 for a large poster.</para><para>The correct answer is $4.95 for a large poster.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_6332fd04-018c-42ef-a40b-82ea1f16e951" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the price of a small poster, \(s\).</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! $2.30 for a small poster.</para><para>The correct answer is $2.30 for a small poster.</para></solution></exercise></section><!--Q9-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Explain or show how you know the prices of the posters.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    
    <para>When the equations</para>
    
    <para>\(2&amp;ell;+3s=16.80\) and \(&amp;ell;+4s=14.15\) are graphed, the intersection of the two lines is at \((4.95,2.30)\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<title>Video: Meeting Constraints</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about constraints. If you have any questions, play the video again or ask
  your teacher for clarification.</para>
